241
00:13:37.659 --> 00:13:41.320
<v 73734075-CCA6-4B68-AABE-3FACDAF4261A>Kelly: Um, but, uh, the bugs for bugs.

242
00:13:39.940 --> 00:13:46.720
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: The, so yeah, invertebrates are not something that is uh, within that realm.

243
00:13:46.720 --> 00:13:57.039
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: Um, I mean, part of that is like, we don't have a good understanding of, like, their, you know, pain, like, how do we know when they're in pain or how do we know?

244
00:13:56.500 --> 00:13:57.002
<v 73734075-CCA6-4B68-AABE-3FACDAF4261A>Kelly: Mm-hmm.

245
00:13:57.039 --> 00:13:59.019
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: Like, we don't have a very good understanding of that.

246
00:13:59.019 --> 00:14:05.200
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: So it's, it's a little difficult to create standards, if we don't have that information.

247
00:14:05.200 --> 00:14:09.279
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: And it's going to be highly dependent, you know, based on what you're studying.

248
00:14:09.279 --> 00:14:17.080
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: However, there are a lot of entomologists, myself included, that think that before you do anything, I do a lot of collecting.

249
00:14:17.200 --> 00:14:24.700
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: And I make sure that whenever I do any collections, I seriously consider what am I going to do with my bugs?

250
00:14:24.700 --> 00:14:36.159
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: Like what, after I collect all these data and then I can say things about, you know, where I caught things, how I caught things, et cetera, et cetera, what's going to happen to all of these insects?

251
00:14:36.159 --> 00:14:44.259
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: And not only the ones that I'm interested in studying, but also the ones that are caught as a consequence of it.

252
00:14:44.259 --> 00:14:56.679
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: So like think of someone who's fishing and they throw a net and they catch all of these fish that they're that are part of their fishery and they're going to sell these fish to their fishery, but they also catch lobsters and sharks.

253
00:14:46.500 --> 00:14:47.502
<v 73734075-CCA6-4B68-AABE-3FACDAF4261A>Kelly: Mm-hmm.

254
00:14:56.679 --> 00:15:00.340
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: And like, you know, other things that are not what their target is.

255
00:15:00.340 --> 00:15:04.960
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: And that happens a lot in insect research as well.

256
00:15:04.960 --> 00:15:10.480
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: So we put traps out like for bees, but these traps don't just collect bees.

257
00:15:10.480 --> 00:15:18.879
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: It's not, you know, it's not like these traps are sitting in the environment and if a beetle comes around, the trap is like, no, you are not chosen.

258
00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:12.139
<v 73734075-CCA6-4B68-AABE-3FACDAF4261A>Kelly: Mm-hmm.

259
00:15:18.879 --> 00:15:32.320
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: So it's something that not a lot of peop, not a lot of people have considered, but it's becoming more common to start thinking about the ethical ramifications of our capture.

260
00:15:32.320 --> 00:15:35.679
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: I think the general consensus is that it's not, it doesn't harm the environment.

261
00:15:35.679 --> 00:15:40.539
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: As long as you don't have those traps out, like in perpetuity, constantly running.

262
00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:40.899
<v 73734075-CCA6-4B68-AABE-3FACDAF4261A>Kelly: Mm-hmm.

263
00:15:40.539 --> 00:15:43.360
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: So a lot of us will do just snapshots.

264
00:15:43.360 --> 00:15:57.820
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: So I would put traps out for maybe a week or maybe like a couple days and I would only have so many traps in the in the area so that I could make the inferences that I want to make from that information.

265
00:15:57.820 --> 00:16:03.580
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: But I won't like just have it in every square inch of, like, I won't have traps everywhere.

266
00:16:03.580 --> 00:16:12.759
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: So, even if you are impacting the population at the area that your trap is, there's still like so much more forest.

267
00:16:12.759 --> 00:16:17.080
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: There's so much more whatever your habitat is that you are not touching.

268
00:16:17.080 --> 00:16:18.502
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: Um,

269
00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:22.059
<v 73734075-CCA6-4B68-AABE-3FACDAF4261A>Kelly: It's almost kind of even like mushroom collecting.

270
00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:22.899
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: Mm-hmm.

271
00:16:22.059 --> 00:16:38.500
<v 73734075-CCA6-4B68-AABE-3FACDAF4261A>Kelly: Where, you know, like people are only supposed to harvest, um, like a section of wild mushrooms that like the rest can continue growing and they're not like just wiping out, uh, an entire area of those mushrooms, um,

272
00:16:27.500 --> 00:16:28.279
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: Mm-hmm.

273
00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:37.899
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: Mm-hmm.

274
00:16:38.620 --> 00:16:48.460
<v 73734075-CCA6-4B68-AABE-3FACDAF4261A>Kelly: But I think that's like a really good approach, like that's, that seems like so, um, I can't even think of that, like, oh, uh, conscious, I guess.

275
00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:48.960
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: Mm-hmm.

276
00:16:48.960 --> 00:16:50.220
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: Yeah.

277
00:16:50.220 --> 00:16:55.379
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: Um, so there's that aspect of it where you consider where you're going to put your traps.

278
00:16:55.379 --> 00:16:56.940
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: Also, depending on where you are.

279
00:16:56.940 --> 00:17:07.440
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: So, If you're in the Midwest, for example, there are some bumblebee species that are considered either endangered or threatened, including the rusty patch bumblebee.

280
00:17:07.440 --> 00:17:11.940
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: And so you actually cannot deploy traps that are indiscriminate.

281
00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:18.839
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: It is illegal to do so in for a great amount of that area.

282
00:17:18.839 --> 00:17:23.940
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: And so you have to completely change your methodology of how you collect these insects.

283
00:17:23.940 --> 00:17:25.200
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: So some people will have nets.

284
00:17:25.200 --> 00:17:32.160
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: They might, you know, there are these vacuums, like think of like a handheld vacuum that you would like vacuum out your car with.

285
00:17:32.160 --> 00:17:38.700
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: We can actually like literally just hack those apart and make something that you can catch bugs with.

286
00:17:38.700 --> 00:17:41.279
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: And that's some of my research was that.

287
00:17:41.279 --> 00:17:58.200
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: I just stood over flowers that I planted in a few different arrangements and at different sites and then went to those sites and just caught all of the bugs that were on a certain flower species. And I was basically like, were the insects that visited these flowers different in these different environments.

288
00:17:58.200 --> 00:18:02.579
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: Um, And so it's just straight up a vacuum.

289
00:18:02.579 --> 00:18:16.619
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: Um, But yeah, so there's, and when we, when we euthanize them as well, we try and do so, um, humanely and ethically is like, we're not just going to let them sit there and just slowly like die in a jar, right?

290
00:18:16.619 --> 00:18:27.059
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: Like you, we either freeze them or we, um, put them in a container that has ethylacetate, um, and it, they just slowly kind of go to sleep.

291
00:18:27.059 --> 00:18:37.619
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: Um, I usually just recommend the freezer or I will like bring a freezer with me to, um, to the field, like a, like dry ice or something like that.

292
00:18:37.619 --> 00:18:40.079
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: I really like dry ice. As an example.

293
00:18:40.079 --> 00:18:46.140
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: Um, so it's, it's, it's kind of an, it depends question.

294
00:18:46.140 --> 00:18:50.099
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: If you are and it depends on like why you are doing what you're doing.

295
00:18:50.099 --> 00:18:54.299
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: So if it's for scientific research, you know, it's generally considered okay.

296
00:18:54.299 --> 00:18:59.819
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: But then even within that community, like what are you doing with your bycatch, what are you doing with the bugs once you're done?

297
00:18:59.819 --> 00:19:01.980
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: Like, are you just literally like, oh, I studied them all?

298
00:19:01.980 --> 00:19:07.440
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: I'm going to toss them in the trash. Or, do you have plans to donate them to an institution in your area?

299
00:19:07.440 --> 00:19:15.779
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: Um, like all of the bugs that I collected for my research here at Penn State are going into the Frost Entomological Museum at Penn State.

300
00:19:15.779 --> 00:19:22.140
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: So they will be there in perpetuity and can be used further, um, future research.

301
00:19:22.140 --> 00:19:40.680
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: Um, and then going to places like I've gone to a couple of things like Beetle school in Arizona and fly school in Costa Rica and there are people that go on those and just kind of are collecting everything that they can because they just and that's, you know, fine as long as they're not collecting every single thing.

302
00:19:40.680 --> 00:19:41.160
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: you know?

303
00:19:41.160 --> 00:19:44.940
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: You want to make sure that you're collecting like a representative.

304
00:19:44.940 --> 00:19:50.640
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: So there were some of these like bright green, shiny beetles, for example, that were there.

305
00:19:50.640 --> 00:19:55.380
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: And a lot of us, we caught like one maybe two.

306
00:19:55.380 --> 00:19:57.720
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: But other than that, we just left them, you know?

307
00:19:57.779 --> 00:20:01.619
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: Because they're endemic to that area.

308
00:19:58.000 --> 00:19:58.502
<v 73734075-CCA6-4B68-AABE-3FACDAF4261A>Kelly: Mm-hmm.

309
00:20:01.619 --> 00:20:05.759
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: They're not super, super common. Or they used to be, but they're not that common anymore.

310
00:20:05.759 --> 00:20:12.359
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: And so you just leave them, um, once you have, however many it is that you want.

311
00:20:12.359 --> 00:20:19.680
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: So I took, there were 3 species, and I took one of each species, but then I took an extra one that I gave to, again, my museum.

312
00:20:19.680 --> 00:20:23.460
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: Um, because my local museum did not have an example of that.

313
00:20:23.460 --> 00:20:26.279
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: So if you're going, if you go somewhere.

314
00:20:26.279 --> 00:20:38.640
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: Like, if you're going to go to Mexico where monarchs have their like mass overwintering grounds and you just start like hoovering up monarchs, like, clearly not a good thing.

315
00:20:38.640 --> 00:20:49.019
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: But, um, if you, and choosing species that might be more sensitive or also clearly not a good thing.

316
00:20:39.500 --> 00:20:40.460
<v 73734075-CCA6-4B68-AABE-3FACDAF4261A>Kelly: Yeah.

317
00:20:49.019 --> 00:21:08.099
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: But there's some really common butterfly species here that if you were to collect a couple of them, like maybe, I don't know, like 15 of them to make a display, not super, something to be super, super concerned about, um, unless you are one of like 100s of people doing so.

318
00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:11.819
<v 73734075-CCA6-4B68-AABE-3FACDAF4261A>Kelly: Yeah, that's that, I mean, that's always the issue.

319
00:21:11.099 --> 00:21:12.359
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: Mm-hmm.

320
00:21:11.819 --> 00:21:13.859
<v 73734075-CCA6-4B68-AABE-3FACDAF4261A>Kelly: Too, um.

321
00:21:13.859 --> 00:21:21.660
<v 73734075-CCA6-4B68-AABE-3FACDAF4261A>Kelly: So what do you think about, like, stuff like, uh, people who take, like, the spotter...

322
00:21:21.660 --> 00:21:24.900
<v 73734075-CCA6-4B68-AABE-3FACDAF4261A>Kelly: Oh my god, a spotted spotted lantern fly.

323
00:21:25.440 --> 00:21:29.460
<v 73734075-CCA6-4B68-AABE-3FACDAF4261A>Kelly: And make like jewellery from the wings.

324
00:21:29.460 --> 00:21:32.880
<v 73734075-CCA6-4B68-AABE-3FACDAF4261A>Kelly: Because, you know, they're on like a kill on site list.

325
00:21:33.000 --> 00:21:33.779
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: Mm-hmm.

326
00:21:33.779 --> 00:21:38.700
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: So that is another example of, like, it depends.

327
00:21:38.700 --> 00:21:44.640
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: So if you were in, if you were in their native range, that'd probably not be a great thing to do, right?

328
00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:43.140
<v 73734075-CCA6-4B68-AABE-3FACDAF4261A>Kelly: Mm-hmm.

329
00:21:43.140 --> 00:21:44.819
<v 73734075-CCA6-4B68-AABE-3FACDAF4261A>Kelly: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

330
00:21:44.640 --> 00:21:52.319
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: But here where we are, especially in like the New York City area, and now where I am in Pennsylvania, they're starting to make their way.

331
00:21:52.319 --> 00:21:58.200
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: Um, Yeah, they, these are insects that are highly damaging to our environment.

332
00:21:52.500 --> 00:21:53.502
<v 73734075-CCA6-4B68-AABE-3FACDAF4261A>Kelly: Oh, no.

333
00:21:58.200 --> 00:22:03.720
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: Um, they basically, we, forever ago, we brought over this tree called Tree of Heaven.

334
00:22:03.720 --> 00:22:08.640
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: that we thought was cool, and we planted it everywhere because people thought it was cool.

335
00:22:08.640 --> 00:22:13.980
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: And then spot and lanternfly came over on a freight ship or something.

336
00:22:13.980 --> 00:22:17.339
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: Um, it was actually in Berks County, Pennsylvania, 2014.

337
00:22:17.000 --> 00:22:20.839
<v 73734075-CCA6-4B68-AABE-3FACDAF4261A>Kelly: Yeah, I thought it, you know, it's funny because I, I, when you said, oh, they're coming to Pennsylvania.

338
00:22:20.839 --> 00:22:23.599
<v 73734075-CCA6-4B68-AABE-3FACDAF4261A>Kelly: I was like, I'm pretty sure it started there, but then it's not.

339
00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:23.200
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: It did.

340
00:22:23.599 --> 00:22:25.099
<v 73734075-CCA6-4B68-AABE-3FACDAF4261A>Kelly: But it's not as big there.

341
00:22:24.640 --> 00:22:26.920
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: No, they didn't move.

342
00:22:26.920 --> 00:22:34.539
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: So they were kind of stuck in like the Philly area for a while, and then they slowly started making their way west.

343
00:22:34.539 --> 00:22:42.700
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: Um, and because we brought all of these trees, tree of heaven, and they're all over the place, it kind of was like, here's all your food.

344
00:22:42.700 --> 00:23:02.259
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: Like we've already, we've already populated all your food, but all of the predators in this environment, all of the like birds that could eat them, things like that, they don't, they have not evolved away to be able to consume spotted lanternfly and like compensate for the toxins.

345
00:22:44.500 --> 00:22:46.359
<v 73734075-CCA6-4B68-AABE-3FACDAF4261A>Kelly: We made your environment for you.

346
00:23:02.259 --> 00:23:20.920
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: So, spot and lantern fly when they eat tree of heaven, they sequester these, um, toxic chemicals that then act as like a protection because a bug will or a bird will try and eat it and will be like, ugh, that's gross and spit it out and then that bird will never try and eat another one.

347
00:23:21.220 --> 00:23:36.640
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: Um, and in, I think where they're from in Korea, like that, there are predators, both vertebrate and invertebrate predator, like other bugs as well, who are just like, well, we've, we have something in our tummies that's going to break that down.

348
00:23:36.640 --> 00:23:39.099
<v ED5485B4-A3ED-4DE0-B9F4-F5EEC0ABBE0E>Codey: And so we don't care at all.

